From 7df3faa859f5adc1b7882682315bd9a2cd1500a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 00:23:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] Video at its own rate with VRR on its own where
 the display has no mode at the video's rate

A display without a mode at the rate of a video leaves Kodi's choice in the mode of the menus, at a
fixed refresh that is no whole multiple of the video's rate. A Samsung Odyssey G60SD
has no mode of 24, 48 or 72 Hz at 2560x1440: 23.976 fps went at 359.999 Hz, one picture a
refresh longer every 2.8 s (at 119.999 Hz, every 8.4 s), in every Hades version (1.0 and
1.1, by their logs). Its VRR range of 48 to 360 Hz shows each picture three times at 71.928 Hz, evenly.
Hades never flipped with VRR before 1.1 (Kodi never set VRR_ENABLED), so no version showed it so.

- After Kodi's own choice (CResolutionUtils::ChooseBestResolution()), where it stays in the mode of
  the menus and that mode is no whole multiple of the video's rate (VRR_VIDEO::Automatic()), the video
  goes at its own rate with VRR without the setting: TakeVideoAtOwnRate() is asked again with
  automatic true, and Decide() says whether VRR can do it, as with the setting (SDR and HDR10,
  progressive, not 3D nor Dolby Vision). Fail-closed: the first flips say whether VRR came on
  (CStartCheck), and where it did not, the video plays in Kodi's choice, as before.
- On NVIDIA that needs VRR in the mode of the menus: the mode sets of the menus leave HDMI VRR to the
  driver (CHdmiVrrOff::Allowed(), autoDisplay), as LibreELEC and Hades 1.0 did, where the display
  declares VRR and no mode at the resolution of the menus is a whole multiple of the rate of films
  (VRR_VIDEO::AutoForDisplay()). A display with modes of 23.976 Hz (the LG C5, TVs) has them: its mode
  sets, and its change of mode for a video, stay as they are.
- kodi.log says it: "goes at its own rate with VRR ... (on its own: the display has no mode at its
  rate ...)", or why not and at which rate it plays.

Not for HDR10+: on Turing and Ampere the VRR packet takes the one InfoFrame slot of HDR10+ (9051), and
Kodi does not know which GPU it drives; Dolby Vision and HLG keep their change of mode.

Checked: the host test (vrr_video_test.cpp: WholeMultipleOf(), Automatic(), AutoForDisplay() with the
modes of the G60SD and of the LG C5, and Decide() for the G60SD); the changed files and the sources
that include them compile with the commands of the image's build. Not run on a GPU.
